Feature: EN 1990 structural scene
  The basis-of-design snapshot and its check report project to a deterministic, locale-free 3D scene, a set of 2D diagrams and drag handles.

  Further specification assertions, asserted by the Rust unit tests of the crate and not by the platform adapters:
  - members span and deflect: Given the default office model When the scene is projected Then entity "beam-B1" is a member of 6.00 m with supports at both ends And its deflected shape peaks at w_net,fin = w1 + w2 + w3 - w_c at mid-span
  - the load glyphs follow the active combination: Given the default office model When the combination "6.10 leading Q-office" is active Then the glyph of "G-sup" has the size 1.35 times 80 kN and the glyph of "Q-office" 1.5 times 40 kN When the combination "6.10 leading Q-wind" is active Then the glyph of "Q-office" has the size 1.5 times 0.7 times 40 kN And an action with the factor zero in the quasi-permanent combination is drawn as a ghost
  - utilisation and reliability badges: Given the default office model and its check report When the scene is projected Then entity "beam-B1" carries the largest utilisation of its own checks And the badges state the consequence class, the reliability class with K_FI, beta against its target and the annex
  - handles write document leaves: Given the default office model When the span handle of "beam-B1" is dragged to 6.012 m Then one write sets "members[id=beam-B1].span" to 6.0 m And the magnitude handle of "Q-office" at one metre height writes 40 kN to "variables[id=Q-office].qk"
  - diagrams agree with the closed forms: Given the default office model and its check report When the diagrams are built Then the matrix repeats the generator factors and hatches the leading action And an independent Python oracle reproduces the gamma and psi factors, the failure probability Phi(-beta), the FORM design point, the Annex D table D1 and the S-N slopes
  - the combination track scrubs the generated columns: Given the default office model and its check report When the scene is projected Then the scene offers one combination track with one keyframe per generated column And every action glyph carries the gamma times psi factor of the action in each column as a field
  - the SLS limit lines bound the elastic deflection line: Given the default office model and its check report When the diagrams are built Then each member with a span ratio shows its net and variable-action deflection lines against the limit lines L over n And the midspan marker is bound to the net deflection check and drags the variable-action deflection w3

  - the live drag re-ranks the combinations: Given the default office model When the magnitude of "Q-wind" is increased step by step through the incremental evaluation Then the ranking diagram lists every combination by descending utilisation And its values equal those of the full evaluation And the governing marker moves to a wind-led combination And every factored effect bar of the envelope follows the dragged magnitude
  - the permanent factor bands follow the combination stepper: Given the default office model When the scene is projected Then the beam carries one colour band per permanent action with one scalar field per combination And the action that adds to the effect shows the unfavourable factor and the relieving action the favourable factor of the column
  - the reliability index is drawn against K_FI: Given the default office model When the reliability class is changed from RC1 to RC3 Then the design point of the K_FI diagram moves to the K_FI of the declared class at the computed beta and stays bound to the Annex C check
  - the three serviceability combinations are drawn side by side: Given a member with criteria C_d for the characteristic, frequent and quasi-permanent combination When the diagrams are projected Then the diagram "sls-combinations:<member>" lists the utilisation E_d / C_d of Eq. 6.14b, 6.15b and 6.16b in non-increasing order, each bar bound to its check, and a combination without a criterion has no bar
  - the FORM sensitivity factors of several actions are drawn as bars: Given a reliability analysis with a second action When the diagrams are projected Then the diagram "form-bars:<analysis>" shows alpha of the resistance upward and of every action downward with the squares summing to one And every diamond is bound to the Annex C check And the member named by the analysis carries a reliability badge with beta and the target
  - the test results are drawn against the fitted line: Given a test series with r_e:r_t pairs and the theory value g_rt(X_m) When the diagrams are projected Then the diagram "test-pairs:<series>" scatters the pairs with the line r_e = b r_t And the markers r_k and r_d are bound to the D.7.2 standard evaluation check
  - the fatigue bins and the member resistances have grips: Given the analysed office model When the selection holds a fatigue bin and a member Then the grips for the detail category, the number of cycles, the design resistances and the serviceability criteria write their document leaves
  - every check is drawn on its owner row or on the project badge: Given any of the example documents and its check report When the check outcomes are routed Then each check resolves to the row its subject names, else to the only row of the collection of its family in the owner table, else to the project badge through a document-level family And the focus of the outcome names that row And the scene draws it
  - every numeric field has a grip: Given the snapshot schema When every numeric field of every row collection is listed Then each is written by exactly one geometric grip, bar grip, move grip or gauge, or is exempt with a reason And a gauge moves its field by twenty schema steps per metre from the current value and writes only leaves of the schema
  - a traffic load group component has a choice grip: Given the office model with two traffic load group rows When the selection holds a component Then the grip "<row>:group" steps through the groups and writes the typed token to "trafficGroups[id=<row>].group"
